WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.120
I'm in India in Mumbai, what used to be called Bombay,

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.900
and behind me is the famous Taj Mahal hotel

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:15.040
And when I'm in India, of course I begin to think about Indian chemistry, Indian science

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.300
and there're really two things which come to my mind

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.660
not necessarily the most important but the ones that really strike me

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:26.960
One is a tragedy and the other's a triumph

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.560
So let's begin with the tragedy

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:36.100
which was the worst chemical accident that's ever occurred

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.160
And it happened in the city of Bhopal which is a long way from here

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.160
It's right in the middle of India

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:48.940
And it was due to discharge of chemicals from a closed chemical factory

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.200
It wasn't even making chemicals

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.240
(They) used to make insecticide but they decided to close it down

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:04.180
And in this factory which was being decommissioned there was a tank of a particular chemical known as methyl isocyanate

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:12.460
Isocyanate is a group which contains nitrogen, carbon and oxygen in a line N-C-O

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:19.660
And methyl isocyanate can react with water to generate heat and to generate gases

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:25.380
And you can imagine the heat plus gas generates pressure and a tank can blow up

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:32.660
And what is worse (is) that one of the gases it can generate is hydrogen cyanide which is very poisonous

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:37.100
and if you breathe it can kill you at really quite a low dose

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.180
So imagine it in the middle of the night this reaction took place

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:55.280
and while it was dark, cloud of... the tank burst, and clouds of poison gas spread across the town across the poorest areas of the town killing thousands of people

00:01:55.660 --> 00:02:03.380
There's still argument whether it was two-and-a-half thousand or 15,000 people but an enormous number and injuring a lot more

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.120
There are several theories how the water got in the tank

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:14.020
ranging from straightforward misfortune to bad management of the plant or even sabotage

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:24.860
and there's even not quite clear exactly what reaction took place because the products of the reaction depend on the temperature

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:30.080
so the proportion of hydrogen cyanide depends on exactly what temperature the tank broke

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:39.000
Hydrogen cyanide is a very powerful poison because the cyanide group can bind to various molecules in the body

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:43.580
One of the symptoms is that your lips apparently go blue

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.460
Fortunately I've never seen anybody who has died from cyanide poisoning

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.600
But it's a very painful death and very quick

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:55.940
So if you're asleep and breathe hydrogen cyanide you would be dead before you knew it

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:02.740
It was a terrible tragedy but it was a wake-up call to the chemical industry across the world

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:06.640
So the suffering of these poor people was not in vain

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:14.020
because nowadays chemical plants are operated far more safely than they were 30 years ago

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:21.440
India is also very famous for one particular physicist Professor Raman

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:31.120
and Professor Raman worked in Culcatta, now called Kolkata, in Bengal in the right ... very east of India

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:34.380
and he was professor of physics at the university there

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.680
and he discovered an effect which to this day is called the Raman effect

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.580
and for which he won the Nobel Prize for Physics in 1930

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:49.040
and the Raman effect is the basis of a very important ...

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:54.860
important sort of spectroscopy that is now used all over the world

00:03:54.860 --> 00:04:03.160
It's really quite simple and it's a technique which is now applied to all sorts of chemical reactions and chemical processes

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:13.840
And it involves shining a beam of light which is of a single color - blue or green or red

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:20.200
and shining it onto a sample and the sample scatters the light

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:25.540
So a large amount of the red light or blue light is scattered back and you can see it

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:33.500
But if you analyze the light very carefully you also find that there is a small proportion of the light

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:38.960
that's had its colour changed at very discrete frequencies that correspond to

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:44.380
the vibrations of the molecule of the substance you're irradiating

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:52.060
And it is these frequencies that give you a signature of the molecule - sort of fingerprints of the molecule

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.320
and allows you to tell what it is

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.440
When Raman did it - his first experiments - he did them here in India where

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.220
the sunshine is very bright; except now, in the monsoon season

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:08.020
and this intense light was enough ... from the sunlight ... to do the experiment

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:16.060
When I was a student, you had to use huge military lamps to get a Raman spectrum and it took several hours

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.520
But after the invention of lasers ...

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:26.260
...then, you could use a laser and record a Raman spectrum in a few seconds or even less

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:30.180
So Raman spectroscopy has become enormously important

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:38.000
You can use it for identifying the pigments - the paints that are used in fine art

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.460
You can tell if something's a forgery

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.840
You can use it for monitoring chemical processes to tell if things are going wrong

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:55.720
And so it is a fantastic discovery that is a credit to India and which is remembered by chemists all over the world

